window.DEVLOG_INDEX = [{"t": "What the crew would cost at API prices", "s": "About $1,200 so far for 33 hours of a fourteen-session crew, if it were billed per token. $577 of that is measured; the rest is an estimate with its working shown. The figure on this site now updates every time it publishes.", "u": "posts/2026-11-13-1347-what-the-crew-would-cost.html", "c": ["Behind the scenes", "Tools and pipeline"], "w": ["Toolsmith", "Director", "Devlog", "Me"], "d": "Day 2, hour 33", "o": 1794577620.0, "i": "media/crew-cost-by-session.thumb.jpg", "x": "I asked the crew to work out what this game would cost if every token were paid for at API prices. It runs on a subscription, so nothing here is billed per token. But it's the number a reader would want, and nobody was recording it until late this morning. Each session's cost at API prices, as Claude Code reports it. The Director and the Devlog run on Fable, the builders on Opus. The running total At API prices, as of 2026-11-13 13:45 UTC. Measured: $577 over 20 sessions. With the estimate for the hours before the ledger: $827 to $2,027, most likely about $1,207. Model (measured) Cost Opus 5.5 $353 Fable 5.2 $223 Sonnet 5.5 $1 Session (measured) Cost director $205 gameplay $153 engine $102 world-2 $37 world $19 devlog $18 gameplay-3 $10 npc-ai $9 Updated every time the devlog publishes. The crew runs on a subscription; nothing here was billed per token. How it's counted Measured. At 11:34 today the toolsmith had every session start writing a row to a ledger every four minutes: role, model, and the cost Claude Code itself reports for that session. Numbers only, nothing from the conversation. A session's figure covers its whole life, not just the time since 11:34. The Director has run since the jam started at 06:00 yesterday, and the engine and the first Gameplay session for 27 hours, so a lot of the jam is in the measured part already. Estimated. Sessions that were closed or restarted before 11:34 left no figure. For those I counted the hours each role was open and multiplied "}, {"t": "The diner has a door", "s": "UFO 01 was impossible at 07:28 because nothing could get into the diner. On the 12:30 reel the autoplayer walks in, finds the fuse, and leaves through the stage door to a CUT! PRINT IT card.", "u": "posts/2026-11-13-1320-the-diner-has-a-door.html", "c": ["World and visuals", "Gameplay"], "w": ["world-2", "UI", "Gameplay", "Engine"], "d": "Day 2, hour 33", "o": 1794576000.0, "i": "media/ufo01-walk-into-the-comet-diner.thumb.jpg", "x": "12:30 reel, Saucers Over Millbrook. Up to the Comet Diner, past the alien on the door, and inside to the fuse box. This morning I wrote \" the first UFO level is impossible to complete as the mesh for the diner doesn't have a way in\". Five hours later the autoplayer does it from the title screen: all four script lines, the exit, the replay, the dailies and on to the next scene. It took three goes to get a hole in a wall. 05:05. A fixed diner mesh was written. It was never imported, so the kit ran and changed nothing. 08:52. The real cause: the kit switched the panel's collision off, and the mesh's own profile switched it back on at every load. 09:30. A filmed walk test proved nothing, because the test's input never reached the player. The rule that came out of it is now in the Director's orders to the whole crew: \"A fix isn't done until it is seen working on the real path.\" The Comet Diner from Main Street. The diamond marker is navigation prototype B, with the distance to the objective. Also on this reel Three more of my notes are answered here. The script page is back, after losing its lines on the last reel, and reads as ink on yellow with finished lines struck through. The current line: \"Inside the diner, the DOUBLE finds the jukebox fuse. (the lit door under the neon)\". Pickups glow and say what to do. The ray gun sits on a crate with a yellow glow and \"RAY GUN · WALK OVER\"; batteries say \"[E] PICK UP\". CUT! PRINT IT. Walking out through the stage door used to give a seco"}, {"t": "The lock nobody was holding", "s": "An empty git lock file has blocked every commit in the shared checkout at least ten times since last night. The tool that's allowed to delete it failed its first review, then starved, then learned to ask who is older.", "u": "posts/2026-11-13-1133-the-lock-nobody-was-holding.html", "c": ["Bloopers", "Behind the scenes"], "w": ["Toolsmith", "Director", "Me"], "d": "Day 2, hour 31", "o": 1794569580.0, "i": "media/stale-locks-watchdog.thumb.jpg", "x": "Most of the crew works in one shared copy of the game's repository. When git writes to it, it drops a small file, index.lock , and removes it when it's done. If the file is still there, every other git command refuses to commit. Since Thursday 23:44 that file has been left behind at least ten times: zero bytes, no git process alive, nobody holding it. Each time, every session's commits failed until someone noticed. From the friction log: \"stale .git/index.lock (0 bytes, 02:13, no git.exe running) blocked every commit in the shared checkout for 4+ min; removed it by hand.\" The watchdog's morning: three reviews, one failure in the field, one better rule. The suspects One was mine. I had GitHub Desktop open on the repository, and its background refresh takes the lock. I closed it at about 07:50. The locks kept coming. The second was the crew's own read-only commands. The Director caught itself: \"a 'git lfs ls-files' held the index lock for minutes\". Those now run with GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0 , which tells git not to take the lock just to look. The real cause of the empty ones is still open. A tool that deletes locks is dangerous The toolsmith's watchdog already sent me an urgent alert when a lock went stale. (At 08:41 I told the Director \"I've got the GitHub alert, you are going to have to try to deal with it\". It was this.) The obvious next step was to let it delete the lock itself. Deleting a lock that is in use can corrupt the repository's index for fourteen sessions at once. So"}, {"t": "Tape on the floor: my playtest notes, three hours on", "s": "Four of this morning's eight playtest notes were on film by 10:00, starting with a trail of tape marks to the objective. Six of the seven levels now finish by their own script.", "u": "posts/2026-11-13-1037-tape-on-the-floor.html", "c": ["Gameplay", "World and visuals"], "w": ["Gameplay", "gameplay-2", "world-2", "World", "Engine"], "d": "Day 2, hour 30", "o": 1794566220.0, "i": "media/tape-trail-back-lot-to-stage-door.thumb.jpg", "x": "I gave the Director eight notes at 07:28. The 09:30 filming block caught the first answers. This is the autoplayer on Zombie 01, on the build the engine merged at 09:26. \"I still find it hard to navigate\" I asked for prototypes, not a fix. The first one is what a film crew would do: tape on the floor. Orange marks lead to wherever the script's current line wants me, and shrink with distance. Navigation prototype A. Tape marks lead across the back lot, past the tanker and through the stage door. The scene cuts to the replay. There's a second prototype behind a switch, a \"where am I going\" key that makes the camera glance at the objective, and a third from the UI session still to come. I'll play all three and pick. \"A simple interaction prompt and a yellow glow\" The shop. KEYS · WALK OVER on the counter; a film can elsewhere says [E] PICK UP. The prompts are in. The glow is built, but I can't see it on this reel, and the keys prompt sometimes shows before the keys are in view. Half done. The same reel also shows a regression: the script page top-left has lost its lines, because a UI kit was only half applied. The \"press G to drop\" prompt for an empty gun passes its test; this level only has a machete, so it isn't on film yet. \"Continuing to run a direction I didn't\" The replay of the same take. The double finishes the last zombie by the truck and walks to the door. The end of the replay looks right. The rest doesn't yet: the crew's independent reviewer counted the double snappi"}, {"t": "Lumen is out: finding seven milliseconds a frame", "s": "My playtest ran at about 20 fps. Within the hour the feel session had measured what every graphics setting costs on Zombie 01, and the Director switched Lumen off for good.", "u": "posts/2026-11-13-0827-where-the-frame-goes.html", "c": ["Bloopers", "Tools and pipeline"], "w": ["gameplay-3", "Director", "Me", "World"], "d": "Day 2, hour 28", "o": 1794558420.0, "i": "media/gpu-cost-zombie01.thumb.jpg", "x": "The last note from this morning's playtest was the bluntest: \"Performance, it's quite bad… I don't mind if we want to go for a non-Lumen approach but we need to be hitting at least 60 fps on this machine.\" I'd played in the editor, at about 20 fps. The editor is slower than the game on its own, but as I told the Director, that \"does seem low even for that\". I can't judge how movement feels at that frame rate, so this came before any more polish. Measure first At 07:36 the Director asked the feel session for a table of what each level costs the graphics card, \"within 60 min\". It wrote a console command, rt.Perf.Sweep , that stands the camera at a level's opening view, switches one setting off, waits, averages three seconds, puts it back and moves to the next. The first table, for Zombie 01, was in at 08:15. What each setting costs on Zombie 01. Lumen's global illumination is the biggest single item; the film look costs 1.1 ms and stays. The frame took 18.7 ms on an RTX 2060. 60 fps needs 16.7. Lumen's lighting was 3.2 ms of that, and switching Lumen from hardware to software ray tracing made it worse, not better. With Lumen off, plain shadow maps and DLSS Quality together: 11.5 ms. The decision The Director took it the same minute: Lumen off in every preset, with screen-space reflections in its place virtual shadow maps off DLSS Quality by default on cards that have it volumetric fog off three or four shadow-casting lights per area, no more the film look stays. The feel sessio"}, {"t": "Playtest two: the enemies turned up, and they aren't smart enough", "s": "I played the build the crew froze at 06:00 after a night on its own. Eight notes came out of it, starting with dumb enemies, a UFO level with no way in, and a frame rate that has to reach 60.", "u": "posts/2026-11-13-0740-playtest-two.html", "c": ["Gameplay", "Behind the scenes"], "w": ["Me", "Director", "Engine"], "d": "Day 2, hour 27", "o": 1794555600.0, "i": "media/ufo01-diner-autoplayer-0530.thumb.jpg", "x": "I went to bed at 22:30 on Thursday with one instruction: a playtest at 07:00, with a report of what changed. The crew stopped adding things at 05:15, fixed blockers only until 06:00, and left me a launcher and a morning report. ( The night shift has what they built.) The report was honest about what it couldn't promise: \"UFO 01 and Noir 01 have never been completed by the autoplayer. Three real blockers were found and fixed in the last hour, none seen on film.\" My eight notes from playtest two, sorted. What I told the Director Last time there were no enemies at all. This time: \"Number 1 piece of feedback is enemies aren't smart enough.\" And one of them is too much the other way: \"The Alien with the shield is a bit too hard to kill.\" One level can't be finished. \"The first UFO level is impossible to complete as the mesh for the diner doesn't have a way in.\" That was one of the three blockers \"fixed in the last hour\". The autoplayer had found a 0.9 m panel across the diner door at about 05:30, the level kit was changed, and nothing walked through the door afterwards. The fix didn't work in practice. The engine session is now finding out what blocks it. UFO 01 on the 05:30 reel: the autoplayer inside the diner with two aliens, on the build before the fix. On mine I couldn't get in. Finding my way. \"It's a lot better but I still find it hard to navigate, maybe we need to prototype some different navigation methods.\" Pickups like the keys \"need a simple interaction prompt and a ye"}, {"t": "The menus, from the design boards to the build", "s": "On Thursday morning Claude Design drew the pause screen and the Dailies card as boards; by the 03:30 reel the UI session had built both, and a polish pass held every screen up against its board.", "u": "posts/2026-11-13-0412-boards-to-build.html", "c": ["Interface"], "w": ["UI", "Engine"], "d": "Day 1, hour 24", "o": 1794543120.0, "i": "media/hold-pause-built-0330.thumb.jpg", "x": "Before After The pause screen. Left: the Claude Design board from Thursday morning. Right: the game at 03:30 Friday. On Thursday morning the UI direction came from a set of Claude Design boards: a call-sheet menu, a HUD for each film, the slate, CUT!, the replay overlay, the Dailies card, and a pause screen called HOLD. Since then the UI session has been turning boards into widgets with kit scripts, one screen at a time. Overnight it was moved up to Opus, and the Director gave it a polish task: every screen I'll see at 07:00, held up against its board, weakest first. Before After The Dailies card. Board on the left, the 01:30 reel on the right: one take, 32.1 seconds against a par of 1:20. The settings screen had a board too, and Claude Design named it the Tech Rider, after the sheet a touring act sends to the venue. The settings sit in three columns, sound, comfort and picture, with a note in the Director's handwriting under them. The Tech Rider. \"No difficulty setting. The director is hard on everyone equally.\" The pause card has its own copy too: \"Clock's stopped. So's the crew's overtime. Take your moment.\""}, {"t": "At 4 a.m. a bot played the level for the camera", "s": "The reels used to fake their kills by console command; at 03:46 a new autoplayer from the gameplay session played Dead Mile Motel with real inputs, died once, finished the script and walked out through the stage door, after staring at the tanker for 86 seconds.", "u": "posts/2026-11-13-0410-a-bot-plays-the-level.html", "c": ["Gameplay", "Tools and pipeline"], "w": ["Gameplay", "Engine", "gameplay-3", "Director"], "d": "Day 1, hour 24", "o": 1794543000.0, "i": "media/autoplayer-hit-flash-back-lot.thumb.jpg", "x": "03:30 reel, back lot. The autoplayer's machete lands: the frame flashes white, then the blood. Until tonight every reel cheated. The recorder put a zombie in front of the camera with a console command and called it a kill. The Director's reviewer kept saying the same thing: the route has never been filmed, and nobody has walked to the keys and the door. So the gameplay session built an autoplayer. rt.Demo.Play presses the same buttons a player would. It walks, turns, swings and follows the script page line by line. The engine used it for the first time in the 03:30 block. Through the shop for the tanker keys. A zombie in teal scrubs is waiting by the shelves. How it went Better than any reel so far, and much funnier. It died once, got CUT, and started take two. Then it cleared the forecourt, found the keys in the shop, cleared the back lot and walked through the stage door into the replay. Every line on the script page was done by play, not by a debug command. Then there's the long pause in the middle. The autoplayer in the back lot, standing very still in front of the tanker. It stands in the back lot facing the tanker with the clock held for 86 seconds, by the independent review's count. Earlier it spent 11 seconds stuck at the half-open shutter. Its log says why: \"stuck N m short; stepping up to it\", four times. It knows where it wants to go, gets close, stops and thinks about it. It's like watching a very polite stunt double. It also earned its keep before it was ever fil"}, {"t": "Too many editors, one machine", "s": "At 02:50 the machine ran out of memory with several headless Unreal runs going at once; it took down the editor and two sessions, and the crew now runs one heavy job at a time.", "u": "posts/2026-11-13-0338-out-of-memory.html", "c": ["Bloopers", "Tools and pipeline"], "w": ["Director", "Engine", "UI", "gameplay-3", "Release"], "d": "Day 1, hour 23", "o": 1794541080.0, "i": "media/out-of-memory.thumb.jpg", "x": "The hour after the crash, from the Director's log. Each crew session can start its own copy of Unreal with no window, to run tests or build. That's how they prove things work without waiting for the one editor everyone shares. Each copy also needs a lot of memory. At 02:50 the main editor crashed in the middle of the UI session's menus kit. Test runs and builds from other sessions were going at the same time, and the machine had run out of memory. When the Director checked round at 03:28, the UI and feel sessions had died in the same moment. They were reopened fresh and carried on from their task logs, which is what those logs are for. The fix was a rule, added to the instructions every session reads: \"One heavy headless job at a time, crew-wide (the editor crashed out of memory at 02:50 Friday with several running): before you start an UnrealEditor-Cmd run (tests, cook, package), check none is already running; if one is, wait and retry every minute.\" It hit the package too The release session's rehearsal package was due at 03:00. At 03:40 its own memory guard stopped it, because with the editor up there wasn't room for both. So the Director changed the morning plan. The 07:00 playtest runs from the standalone launcher. The package gets the machine to itself from about 06:05, with the editor closed and a hard stop at 06:50. Filming slipped from 03:30 to about 04:00. Gameplay-3 had been mid-task; its branch and task log came through intact."}, {"t": "The night shift: how the crew works while I sleep", "s": "I signed off at 22:30 with a playtest booked for 07:00; every two hours since, the engine has filmed every level, a reviewer has marked what's broken, and the Director has sent the fixes out before the next block.", "u": "posts/2026-11-13-0238-the-night-shift.html", "c": ["Behind the scenes"], "w": ["Director", "Engine", "UI", "Gameplay", "gameplay-2", "gameplay-3", "World", "world-2", "NPC AI", "Audio", "Me"], "d": "Day 1, hour 22", "o": 1794537480.0, "i": "media/night-shift.thumb.jpg", "x": "The night so far, from the Director's log. At 22:12 I told the Director I was going to sleep, and asked for a report at 07:00 of what changed. My last message, at 22:30: \"just because it's night mode you're still the boss; if you think the UI needs to step it up a gear, bump the model; that goes for everything.\" The Director's night plan to the crew was ten lines. The first one: \"The human is asleep; never wait for an answer: decide, note the assumption on the task, keep going.\" The loop The night runs on a two-hour loop. On the hour the engine merges the C++ that's ready. At the filming block it records every level from the title screen, with sound. The Director gives those reels to a separate reviewer that hasn't seen the work, so it isn't marking its own homework. Its findings go to the session that owns each one, and the fixes have to land before the next block. The first review, at 23:58, opened with this: \"a first-time player could not yet play L_Zombie_01 from the title to the stage door without being told.\" The Director used the authority I'd given it two minutes later. It moved the UI session up to Opus for the night to wire the script page. Twelve items, two hours later The second review, at 02:15, went back through the same twelve items one by one. Summed up: Since 23:30 Items Fixed Objectives progress, script page in the levels, kill confirm Mostly fixed Replay: the red stunt double now stars, but its head and hands are still the mannequin's Partly The route, deat"}, {"t": "Scene three, from the slate to the dailies", "s": "The 01:30 reel runs Dead Mile Motel end to end for the first time: the script page ticks its lines off, the stage door opens, and the replay stars the stunt double instead of a mannequin.", "u": "posts/2026-11-13-0140-scene-three-slate-to-dailies.html", "c": ["Gameplay", "Interface"], "w": ["UI", "gameplay-2", "gameplay-3", "Gameplay", "NPC AI", "Engine"], "d": "Day 1, hour 21", "o": 1794534000.0, "i": "media/stunt-double-exits-stage-door.thumb.jpg", "x": "01:30 reel. The end of the replay: the stunt double runs for the stage door and out into the light, then the Dailies card. At 23:30 the Director's review was blunt: a first-time player couldn't get through this level without being told what to do. The script page was a static picture, the replay showed a plain white mannequin, and nobody had filmed the route. Two hours later the first two are fixed on film. The route still isn't. The script page does its job The UI session moved up to Opus for the night to wire the page to gameplay-2's objectives system. Now the current line gets a red caret. When it's done it gets struck through and the next one comes up, sometimes with a stage direction under it as a hint. Line one, with the caret: the DOUBLE clears the forecourt. Line two, in the shop, with a hint in brackets: slide under the half-open shutter. Near the end: the finished lines struck through. The replay has a star Gameplay-3 wired the stunt double into the replay: the player's body, built by the NPC session in Blender from a script, with the machete in hand. The red tracksuit is in for now. Red or teal is mine to pick in the morning. The replay opens on a crane shot of the whole gas station. The stunt double in the replay, machete in hand, by the shop. Dailies: one take, 32.1 seconds against a par of 1:20, three stars, \"Keep it. Print it. Next set-up.\" What the reel doesn't prove The recorder still stages its kills by console command. It cleared line one for real, then a d"}, {"t": "Three sets rebuilt in an evening", "s": "After the first playtest the Director had the zombie, UFO and noir levels rebuilt as three connected spaces each, with props made by script in Blender and a lighting pass, and the 23:30 reels show all three.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-2326-three-sets-rebuilt.html", "c": ["World and visuals", "Behind the scenes"], "w": ["world-2", "World", "Engine", "Director", "Me"], "d": "Day 1, hour 19", "o": 1794525960.0, "i": "media/noir01-rebuilt-jazz-club.thumb.jpg", "x": "Dead Mile Motel, Sc. 3. The forecourt, first of the gas station's three spaces. The shop behind it hasn't been filmed yet. Saucers Over Millbrook, Sc. 4. Main Street, lit by the crashed saucer down the road. Rain on Calloway Street, Sc. 2. The jazz club, always black and white. At the first playtest each level was one open space, and I said so. That evening the Director put a different plan on the board: every level becomes three connected spaces, each with a job, joined by a route that warm light pulls you along, and ending at a stage door. Who did what World-2 rebuilt the levels. The gas station became a forecourt, a shop and a back lot. The UFO level became a street, an open-top diner and a back lot under a second saucer. The noir level became a foyer and bar, a club with a mezzanine, and a back office out to an alley. Every one is a kit script, so the engine can rerun it whenever the layout changes. Between 22:18 and 22:25 it did the second level of each film the same way, and the world session lit each one within minutes. The shop needed things to put in it. World-2 wrote a script that builds the props in Blender with no window open, and renders a preview of each before the engine imports them. All under 3,000 triangles, and no brand names. Counter with a till, built by script in Blender. Drinks fridge. Its cold glow lights the back aisle. Shelf unit. The reviewer said it reads a bit like a bookcase. Chest freezer. The world session followed with lighting. My note at 21:"}, {"t": "The machete hit, now the zombie feels it", "s": "After the first playtest I said the zombies didn't seem to feel a hit; by the 23:30 reel a new feel session had added a freeze on contact, a flash, a camera shove, a flinch and blood.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-2325-the-hit-answers-back.html", "c": ["Gameplay", "Characters and NPCs"], "w": ["gameplay-3", "NPC AI", "Audio", "Engine", "Me"], "d": "Day 1, hour 19", "o": 1794525900.0, "i": "media/zombie01-machete-kill-pump.thumb.jpg", "x": "23:30 reel, Dead Mile Motel. One machete kill by the pumps: the zombie flinches, blood hits the pump, the body drops. At the first playtest the machete went through zombies like they were air. What I told the Director afterwards ended up in a task word for word: \"We'll be able to hit the zombie. They won't seem like they felt it, right? We want some juice, maybe a bit of a screen effect of sorts... because we can't have sheer quantity of game because it's a game jam, polish and juice are so key.\" The Director did two things with that. It added a line to the rules every session reads, \"Juice is part of done\", and it started a new session, gameplay-3, whose only job is how things feel. Its first task was the hit. What a hit does now Gameplay-3 took over the feel code an earlier session started (hit-stop, camera kick, a slow beat on the last kill) and added a screen layer on top. On contact the frame freezes for a moment, a white splice flash goes off, the picture slips in the gate like a film jumping a sprocket, and the view is shoved along the swing. All of it sits in data, per weapon, so it can be tuned without touching code. The other half came from mail. Gameplay-3 asked the NPC session for a flinch and blood, audio for a sound per weapon, and the UI session for a hit marker on the crosshair. The flinch and the blood are in this reel. The sounds and the hit marker are built and riding the next merges. Seconds after the kill: blood on the pump and the floor, the body down be"}, {"t": "Edits that undid themselves", "s": "Two sessions saw uncommitted scripts snap back to the last commit; the toolsmith traced it to the engine's merge stashes and one blanket git checkout, and a guard hook now refuses both.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-2212-edits-that-undid-themselves.html", "c": ["Bloopers", "Behind the scenes"], "w": ["Toolsmith", "Director", "Audio", "Engine", "NPC AI", "world-2"], "d": "Day 1, hour 18", "o": 1794521520.0, "i": "media/shared-checkout-reverts.thumb.jpg", "x": "Most of the crew works in one shared copy of the game's files. Only the C++ sessions get their own. That saves disk and build time, and it means one careless git command reaches everyone. Thursday evening in four steps: the symptom, two causes, and the guard that stops both. The symptom At 20:50 the NPC session logged this in FRICTION.md: \"an uncommitted edit to Scripts/NPC/Characters/blender_character.py in the MAIN checkout reverted to HEAD by itself mid-task (no reflog or stash entry)\" Two minutes later World-2 reported the same thing on its level kit. It rewrote the file from memory and committed straight away. The Director opened T-0105 for the toolsmith at P0: find what's doing it, and stop it. Two causes, both innocent alone The first was the engine's merge routine. Before merging a C++ branch it stashed the working tree and popped it back afterwards. In a private checkout that's a normal habit. In a shared one it lifts every session's unsaved work out for the length of the merge, and anything written in that window is lost. The toolsmith found five of those stash pairs in git's loose objects, going back to 11:17. The second was tidier still. The audio session's clean-up tool rewrites a few hundred sound files it didn't mean to change. To undo that, audio ran one command that put back every modified file in the tree. It did that at 20:42 and 20:52. That's the one that took the two kit edits. The fix The toolsmith wrote a guard hook. In the shared copy it now refuses gi"}, {"t": "Milestone two: the core loop plays, in grey boxes", "s": "Seventeen hours in, Reel Time plays from slate to dailies by the path a player takes. At 21:41 it didn't, and the Director refused to call the milestone met.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-2205-greybox-core-loop.html", "c": ["Milestones", "Gameplay"], "w": ["Me", "Director", "Engine", "Gameplay", "gameplay-2", "NPC AI", "World", "world-2", "UI", "Audio", "Floater", "Toolsmith", "Release"], "d": "Day 1, hour 18", "o": 1794521100.0, "i": "media/day-one-take-slate-kills-cut-retake.thumb.jpg", "x": "The second milestone is \"Playable greybox core loop\", due at 23:00 on day one. Its test, written before the jam: \"Greybox level, the core action works, debug commands work, captures flowing.\" Dead Mile Motel at 17:30. Slate, ACTION, two machete kills, a zombie gets through, CUT, and take two. Where it stands All four parts of the test are met, an hour before the deadline. Greybox level. Seven of them: a tutorial, two zombie, two UFO and two noir. The gas station has moved past grey boxes already. The core action works. Time runs only when you move. Machete, ray gun and revolver. One hit is CUT, and the retake follows. Clear the scene and you get the Director's Cut replay and the dailies. Debug commands work. The whole game can be driven from the console, which is how the Engine session films it. Captures flowing. Single captures at 07:30, then six sets of whole-level reels from 11:30. The footnote Half an hour ago this milestone was not met, and the Director said so. Its message to me at 21:41: \"The only time a person played it, no enemy spawned in any level… If not, publish at about 22:50 as 'missed by one bug at the deadline'… That is a better Glass Box post than a tidy 'met'.\" The bug was in the journey between levels, which no reel had ever filmed. So the bar for \"met\" became a journey. First a headless test that starts at the title and travels through all six scenes, checking for a live enemy within ten seconds of ACTION in each. Then the Engine did the same trip in the "}, {"t": "From a playtest note to a script page, in an hour", "s": "I said the game needs objectives, styled like a movie script. Within the hour it was a contract for the crew, three Claude Design boards and seven tasks.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-2052-script-page.html", "c": ["Interface", "Behind the scenes"], "w": ["Me", "Director", "UI"], "d": "Day 1, hour 16", "o": 1794516720.0, "i": "media/ui-mockup-script-page-in-game.thumb.jpg", "x": "The biggest gap from tonight's playtest was that the game never tells you what to do. My note was one sentence: maybe style the objectives like a movie script, so the player is reading the script. This is where that sentence was an hour later. The script page in place, top left under the slate. A mockup, not a screenshot. The film title on it is a placeholder. The idea Each level is a scene, and a scene has a page. The page has a heading, EXT. GAS STATION - NIGHT , and three or four lines of action. Each line is an objective. The current one is highlighted. Finished ones get struck through in pencil. The player is always \"The DOUBLE\". The Director wrote that down as a contract before any code existed, so every session could build against the same thing at once. The contract opens with my own notes quoted back at me, then turns them into data. One level's script is a list of lines, and each line has a type: clear an area, find an item, reach a point, survive, exit. It also covers the other things I asked for. The red X is gone: \"the stage door lights up: a door frame in the set wall with a lit EXIT sign and bright stage light pouring through.\" And so is any idea of a compass: \"A follow spot (a studio spotlight on the rig) swings to point at whatever the current line is about. No compass, no floor mark.\" Three looks While the Director wrote the contract, I took the idea to Claude Design. Three treatments of the same page, each tested over a dark set and a bright one. A is paper"}, {"t": "An experiment: Reel Time builds for the browser", "s": "I asked for a browser-playable build as a side experiment. Unreal 5.9 produced one in under three hours: 110 MB, about ten seconds to load. There's no link yet, because no person has seen it draw a frame.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-2050-web-build-experiment.html", "c": ["Tools and pipeline"], "w": ["Release", "Director", "Me"], "d": "Day 1, hour 16", "o": 1794516600.0, "i": "media/web-build-experiment.thumb.jpg", "x": "At 07:05 this morning I floated a stretch idea to the Director: a playable build on this site, using Unreal 5.9's web packaging. It was logged as a stretch, not to be tried before the first Windows build. The Windows build passed at 19:00, so the Release session got an hour's box to try. The web build in numbers, and what isn't known yet. It was nearly there already The first finding, from the Director's log at 18:25: \"our engine HAS a Wasm platform (Engine/Platforms/Wasm, WebGPU); only the Emscripten SDK 6.0.10 is missing.\" So this wasn't a port. It was one missing download and one build quirk: the usual packaging tool refused to compile while the editor had Live Coding on, so the Release session called the compiler directly. What came out A Shipping build in WebAssembly, drawing with WebGPU. Three levels: the front end, the screen test tutorial and Zombie 01. 110.6 MB as a zip. 51 MB over the network once compressed. Ready in about ten seconds from a cold start on the dev machine. It needs Chrome or Edge 113 or newer. Why there's no button to press yet Two reasons, both from the Release session's handover note. Nobody has seen it. The automated browser reports the game as ready, with no errors. Every screenshot it takes is black. The note's own words: \"Likely a headless WebGPU capture limit, but unconfirmed\". After today's wrong-window reels and my enemy-free playtest, \"likely\" isn't enough. I'll open it in a real browser myself first. It can't live on this site as it is. A"}, {"t": "I played it for the first time, and no enemies showed up", "s": "While the crew was stopped by the third usage stall I played the game for an hour. Every reel today had zombies in it. My playthrough had none, and that wasn't the only thing I'd change.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-2045-playtest-one.html", "c": ["Gameplay", "Behind the scenes"], "w": ["Me", "Director"], "d": "Day 1, hour 16", "o": 1794516300.0, "i": "media/playtest-one-notes.thumb.jpg", "x": "The third usage stall hit at 19:07. The crew stopped. The editor didn't, so I sat down and played Reel Time for the first time, from about 19:10 to 20:20, with nobody to report bugs to. My notes from playtest one, sorted. The blocker Fourteen hours of reels, tests and reviews all showed enemies. Then a person played it. From my notes to the Director when it came back: \"In each of the levels, no enemy spawned at any point. It was just a case of basically walking to the X each time.\" And sometimes not even that: \"sometimes I wouldn't be near the X, or I wouldn't necessarily know where the X is, and it would end anyway.\" An hour before I sat down, the Engine had checked every level and logged enemies spawning in all six. I don't know yet why my run was different. That went to the Gameplay session as the top task the minute the crew was back. It's the same lesson as the HUD this morning and the replay this afternoon, one step further along. A test passed, a reel looked right, and the thing a player does first didn't work. What I liked \"I thought the UI was good.\" The replay camera \"is decent\", though I want it a bit further from the player. \"The theming and styles and art is all fine. It's all actually fairly decent. It doesn't obviously need a big art pass.\" That last one changes the plan more than it sounds. The next push is pacing, not polish. What I asked for A script, not a quest log. The game doesn't tell you what to do. My note: \"maybe we can style it like a movie script, "}, {"t": "The set reacts: a tractor beam and an exploding barrel", "s": "Each film gets one thing in the set you can use against the enemies. The saucer's tractor beam and the gas station's barrels work on film; the noir chandelier falls in a room too dark to see.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-1840-the-set-reacts.html", "c": ["World and visuals", "Gameplay"], "w": ["World", "world-2", "Engine"], "d": "Day 1, hour 14", "o": 1794508800.0, "i": "media/saucer-drops-tractor-beam-on.thumb.jpg", "x": "Every film in Reel Time has a set piece that fights for whoever triggers it first. Saucers Over Millbrook. The saucer over Main Street takes a hit, switches on its tractor beam and sinks toward the street. What each one is meant to do From the task the Director wrote for the second World session: \"UFO: a hovering saucer whose tractor beam sweeps the street on a path during the peak; anything hittable inside is lifted and dropped (killing enemies; the player is CUT if caught), and the player can trigger it early by shooting its emitter.\" The noir film gets a chandelier you shoot down onto whoever is under it. The zombie film gets gas pumps and barrels that blow up. The beam on, seen from under the saucer. The HUD has changed colour too: each film now has its own accent. The barrel Dead Mile Motel. A barrel by the diner takes a hit and goes up. The blast is a placeholder dome for now. The blast, a moment in. It's a plain dome because the effects pass hasn't happened yet. What these clips don't show The Engine's notes are careful about this, and each one ends the same way: \"No zombie close to it in frame, so the knock-down isn't shown.\" \"No alien under the beam in frame.\" \"No gangster under it in frame.\" So what's on film is each set piece going off. None of them is shown doing its job, which is killing something. Their task notes say they do. After today I trust that about as far as the next reel. The chandelier isn't here at all. It falls, but the jazz club is so dark in that "}, {"t": "Eight rules the crew wrote for itself on day one", "s": "The crew started the jam with a rulebook written before the clock. By the evening it had eight new rules, and every one came from something breaking.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-1830-rules-from-breakages.html", "c": ["Behind the scenes", "Bloopers"], "w": ["Director", "Engine", "Toolsmith", "Me"], "d": "Day 1, hour 14", "o": 1794508200.0, "i": "media/rules-written-today.thumb.jpg", "x": "The crew's rulebook was written before the jam, by guessing what would go wrong. Day one replaced the guesses with what did. What broke on the left, the rule it turned into on the right. How a rule gets made The pattern was the same each time. Something breaks. A session reports it. The Director works out the cause, writes one line in its log, and mails the rule to everyone it affects. Usually inside the hour. The filming blocks were mine. By 07:00 the Engine session was behind: there's one Unreal editor, and every session wanted its work filmed. The Engine asked for a second editor. I said no, film in batches. The Director turned that into a timetable. By 10:50 the timetable wasn't enough. From the Director's log: \"34 tasks awaiting the engine, crew idle. Changed captures to 8 integration reels per block (covers many tasks each)\" That one change did more than save time. Filming whole levels is what showed that nothing put the HUD on screen, and that a filmed take never reached its replay. Single-task captures would have passed every one of those tasks. The ones about trust Two of the rules exist because a session said something worked and it didn't. At 08:13 a session on a lighter model reported a task done. The Director checked: \"the file doesn't exist and nothing was committed\". Work from the lighter models already needed a review before it counted. After that the Director's note to itself was \"Watch toolsmith reports closely.\" And at 11:30 the reels that were meant to pro"}, {"t": "The first packaged build had no levels in it", "s": "The game built, zipped and ran with DLSS on a day ahead of the packaging milestone. Then the Release session's own check found only the menu level was inside.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-1820-first-package-no-levels.html", "c": ["Tools and pipeline", "Bloopers"], "w": ["Engine", "Release", "Director"], "d": "Day 1, hour 14", "o": 1794507600.0, "i": "media/first-package-empty.thumb.jpg", "x": "Everything in this devlog so far was filmed inside the Unreal editor. A jam entry is a zip that runs on someone else's PC, and packaging is the step that tends to go wrong on the last night. So the first package was a day-one task, and I'm glad it was. Four steps from \"won't build\" to a fixed cook list, in under three hours. It wouldn't build The editor build worked all day. The packaged game target failed. The cause was DLSS again: the plugin as installed has nothing prebuilt for a packaged game. The Engine's fix was to build DLSS and Streamline as plugins of the project. One decision came with it. The NVIDIA plugin stays out of the game's git repo and a setup script copies it in. The Director's note on why is two words: \"licence caution\". It built, and it was fat At 16:30 the package built: 680 MB zipped. 516 MB of that was debug files. The good news inside it: DLSS reports as on in the packaged game, which matters for the NVIDIA award I'm entering. It was empty The Release session then ran its own pipeline in its own checkout and got the zip down to 472 MB. It had also written a check for which levels are in the package. The answer, from the Director's log at 17:05: \"Correction: the baseline package had no levels cooked (only L_FrontEnd)\" The game opens its levels by name at run time. Unreal's packager only includes levels something points at, and nothing pointed at them. The package would have started, shown the title screen, and had nowhere to go. The fix is a list of ma"}, {"t": "The gas station gets dressed, and the machete gets its first kills on film", "s": "Sixteen script-built props turned the grey-box forecourt into a 1970s gas station, and the 17:30 reel has the first machete kills, the first real death and the first sound.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-1745-set-dressed-first-kills.html", "c": ["World and visuals", "Gameplay"], "w": ["World", "world-2", "Floater", "NPC AI", "Engine"], "d": "Day 1, hour 13", "o": 1794505500.0, "i": "media/machete-kills-at-the-pumps.thumb.jpg", "x": "Two things the reviewer said were missing from the last reel are in this one: a machete kill on a zombie, and a death that wasn't forced by a console command. Dead Mile Motel, take one. Walk in to the pumps, two zombies walk up, and the machete drops them both. 'Yes! That's the stuff!' is the Director. The set Before After The same spot at 12:25 and at 17:30. Boxes became pumps, a canopy, a wrecked sedan, a tanker, a fence and a diner. The World session built the props the same way the NPC session built the cast: a script, run in Blender with no window open. Sixteen meshes, about 17,000 triangles between them, and no textures at all. The colour is one grime material. The second World session then swapped them into the level, keeping a grey box as a fallback for any piece that fails to load. Two zombies at the pump, a swing in progress. These are the scripted bodies from this afternoon. Take two. A zombie ragdolls off to the left, past the tanker. The subtitle is the Director moving on. What's real in this reel and what's staged The Engine's note is exact about it, so I will be too. Real: a zombie hits the player while time is running, and that hit is what triggers CUT and take two. No kill command was used. Staged: the two machete kills. The filming script sets them up through a command called rt.Reel.StageKill . The Engine's note calls them real kills; the staging is the script's. Skipped: the walk to the mark. The script teleports there. So it's closer, and it still isn't a"}, {"t": "Bullets you can watch: the ray gun and the revolver in slow time", "s": "Each film has its own gun. Shots are real objects in the world, so when you stand still they hang in the air with everything else.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-1740-bullets-you-can-watch.html", "c": ["Gameplay", "World and visuals"], "w": ["Gameplay", "gameplay-2", "World", "Engine"], "d": "Day 1, hour 13", "o": 1794505200.0, "i": "media/ray-gun-bolts-hang-in-slow-time.thumb.jpg", "x": "Reel Time's one rule is that time only runs while you move. Guns are where that rule gets interesting. Saucers Over Millbrook. Three shots from the ray gun, then the player stops, and the bolts hang in the street. The HUD's bottom right corner flips between HELD and ROLLING. The task for the time system puts numbers on it: the world runs at about 4% when you stand still, about 15% when you only turn your head, and full speed when you move. Nothing snaps; it eases over a tenth of a second or so. The player is never slowed. A shot isn't a line drawn from the gun to the target. It's an object that flies, so it obeys the same clock. Fire, stop moving, and you can walk your eyes along your own bullets. Enemy shots hang the same way, which is the point: you get to read the room. Two green bolts in mid-air, 1 second 17 frames into the take. The ammo counter reads 21 of 24. One gun per film The level's film picks the weapon you start with: Zombie grindhouse: the machete. 1950s UFO invasion: the ray gun, 24 shots. Noir: a six-shot revolver. There's no reloading. From the Gameplay session's log: \"empty guns just click\". The noir club in black and white. The revolver's rounds show as bright streaks, and the HUD reads ROLLING ×0.16: the world at about a sixth of full speed. The revolver, three rounds left, one in the air on the right. Fixed since this morning At 11:00 the ray gun's glow washed out half the screen and the revolver's shots were invisible. The Engine's note on the stills th"}, {"t": "The Director's Cut: the replay finally shows up on film", "s": "Four sets of reels failed to show a full take with its replay, the point of the game. The fifth does. The cause wasn't the game at all: the filming script was skipping it two seconds in.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-1605-directors-cut-on-film.html", "c": ["Gameplay", "Bloopers"], "w": ["Gameplay", "gameplay-2", "Floater", "UI", "Engine"], "d": "Day 1, hour 12", "o": 1794499500.0, "i": "media/directors-cut-first-replay-on-film.thumb.jpg", "x": "Reel Time's hook is that after every scene you watch your own take back, shot from film cameras. A headless test has said it works since 08:14. Until this reel, I hadn't seen it in a full take. The player hits the mark, the Director says 'Right on the X. Good.', and the screen cuts to DIRECTOR'S CUT: the take played back from two cameras, with a REC timecode and a skip prompt. It's short because the take was short. The filmed take lasted 2.2 seconds, so that's all there is to replay. The figure is the player's stand-in, seen from outside for the first time. The second replay camera, a high crane shot over the forecourt. Frame corners, REC light and timecode are the UI session's replay overlay. Hitting the mark. Two zombies down by the pumps, and the Director's line at the bottom. Take two now gets its own slate. That was missing at 13:30. Why it took five reels 11:30 : the recorder filmed the wrong window. 11:45 and 12:25 : the take never ended properly. A debug \"god mode\" setting had stayed on between play sessions, so the player couldn't die: no CUT, and no dailies. 13:30 : CUT and dailies showed up, and the replay was skipped. 15:30 : this one. The last cause is my favourite. The Director's log at 14:12: \"Found why no reel showed the replay: smoke_run.py skips it 2 s in.\" The script that plays the game for the camera was written as a smoke test. Smoke tests want to be fast, so it pressed skip. The replay had been working, and the thing filming it kept cutting it off. The f"}, {"t": "The cast: five bodies, built by script in under half an hour", "s": "Every enemy was Unreal's grey mannequin this morning. The NPC session built three zombies, a gangster and an alien with MakeHuman in headless Blender, all on the mannequin's skeleton.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-1355-the-cast.html", "c": ["Characters and NPCs", "Tools and pipeline"], "w": ["NPC AI", "Engine"], "d": "Day 1, hour 9", "o": 1794491700.0, "i": "media/cast-closing-in.thumb.jpg", "x": "At 11:00 every enemy in Reel Time was Unreal's plain mannequin. This is the enemy test map at 13:40. All nine enemy types walk in at full speed. Zombies on the left, aliens in the middle, noir gangsters on the right. The glowing balls are enemy shots. The end of that clip. The big one in the apron is the butcher, the lead zombie. How they were made Nobody sculpted anything. The NPC session used MakeHuman, through its Blender plugin MPFB, and ran Blender with no window open. A script sets the body shape, adds clothes, and bakes the make-up into the textures. The trick that made it fast is the skeleton. From the NPC session's own log line at 11:42: \"…built by script in Blender and skinned straight onto Epic's mannequin skeleton, so every existing animation and the ragdoll just work.\" So there was no retargeting and no new animation. The enemy behaviour from this morning carried over untouched. The first zombie was committed at 11:33. By 11:59 the same script, made generic, had produced the rest: The line-up at the start of the clip: three zombies, two aliens behind the pillars, three men in suits and hats. Three zombies: the shambler, a runner in shorts and a vest, and the butcher. A gangster in a suit and fedora, for the noir film. An alien with big eyes, for the UFO film. Five bodies cover nine enemy types. A grunt and an officer share a body and differ in tint, gear and behaviour. What's in the credits The MakeHuman base mesh and body shapes are CC0, and they're listed on th"}, {"t": "CUT! Take two. Print it.", "s": "The 13:30 reel shows most of a take for the first time: zombies walk in, one hit ends it with a CUT card, take two starts at once, and the scene ends on a dailies card.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-1340-cut-take-two-print-it.html", "c": ["Gameplay", "Interface"], "w": ["Gameplay", "UI", "NPC AI", "Engine"], "d": "Day 1, hour 9", "o": 1794490800.0, "i": "media/cut-card-and-instant-retake.thumb.jpg", "x": "Reel Time's loop is a film shoot. One hit and the Director yells cut. You go again straight away. Clear the scene and you get your dailies. Until this reel I'd only seen the first few seconds of that. Take one at the gas station. Two zombies walk in, the player is killed, CUT! slams down, and take two is rolling a few seconds later. The retake is meant to be instant. This morning's headless test measured it at 0.33 seconds; on film the CUT card stays up for a couple of seconds first. Take one, rolling. These two zombies are the new bodies from the NPC session, not mannequins. The CUT card, from the UI session's rebuild of the Claude Design mockup. Dailies: takes, running time against par, three stars, and NEXT SCENE, WATCH AGAIN or RETAKE. The dailies Scene cleared. The dailies card comes up and PRINT IT! stamps the corner. Clear the scene and the Director's line comes up, \"Yes! That's the stuff!\", then this card. It reads from real data: two takes, par of 1:15, three stars. How it was played Nobody held a controller. The Engine session can't press keys, so it drives the game with console commands on a timeline. This is the start of the script for this reel: 7.7s cmd rt.Player.Launch 500 0 13.8s shot hero_01 18.2s cmd rt.Debug.KillPlayer 18.8s shot cut_01 31.4s cmd rt.Flow.Clear So this proves the flow, not the feel. The feel is for tonight, when I play it myself. What the Engine's note says is wrong The Engine wrote up its own reel, including what it didn't see: The CUT card"}, {"t": "An hour later: a slate, a HUD and ACTION!", "s": "At 11:15 no level had a HUD. By 12:25 a take opens with a clapperboard, the Director's line and a film-strip HUD, after three tries at filming it.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-1240-slate-hud-action.html", "c": ["Interface", "Bloopers"], "w": ["UI", "Gameplay", "gameplay-2", "Engine", "Director"], "d": "Day 1, hour 8", "o": 1794487200.0, "i": "media/hud-slate-dead-mile-motel-take-one.thumb.jpg", "x": "The last post ended with thirteen stills and no HUD in any of them. This is the same forecourt 70 minutes later. Take one of Dead Mile Motel. The clapperboard drops in, the Director calls the scene, and the HUD stays: scene card top left, a film-strip timecode along the bottom. That's the first time the game has looked like the mockups. The design came from Claude Design this morning; the UI session rebuilt it as real widgets. The slate. Film title, scene, take, and the Director's subtitle underneath. The same set once the slate clears. The grain, vignette and scratch line are the grindhouse look, now on in play. Saucers Over Millbrook. The HUD picks up the ray gun's ammo count, bottom right. What was missing Nothing was wrong with the HUD. Nothing in the game ever created it. So the Director made one task, top priority: a UI manager that puts the right widget on screen for each stage of a take. The Gameplay session committed it at 11:42 and the Engine merged it at 11:50. The UI session then hooked the slate and CUT card to the same take stages. At 12:12 the Engine reran every kit on top. The run moves on to the next film. The forecourt cuts to the slate for Saucers Over Millbrook, the Director calls ACTION, and the ray gun HUD takes over. Three tries to film it Getting this on film took three sets of reels. 11:30. The clips for three levels recorded the editor's Message Log window for their whole length. Not one frame of the game. It's the same mistake as the very first capt"}, {"t": "Thirteen stills of the game so far: five usable, eight broken", "s": "I asked for screenshots of whatever exists. The UFO and noir sets have their film looks and the ray gun fires, but no level has a HUD, and the stills showed why.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-1130-thirteen-stills-five-usable.html", "c": ["World and visuals", "Bloopers"], "w": ["Engine", "World", "world-2", "Gameplay", "Director", "Me"], "d": "Day 1, hour 7", "o": 1794483000.0, "i": "media/ufo-set-technicolor-look.thumb.jpg", "x": "After the two-hour stall I wanted to see the game, not read about it. So at 11:02 I asked the Director for screenshots of whatever's available, even if it disrupted the queue. The Engine played each level and took thirteen stills. Its verdict: five usable, eight broken. The five The UFO set, \"Saucers Over Millbrook\", in its 1950s Technicolor look. The stack of boxes at the bottom is the ray gun. The sets are still grey boxes. What's new is that each film has its own look on top: the World session's kit adds one per era. The noir set, a jazz club, in black and white with film grain. The exposure is blown out at the edges; that's on the fix list. The ray gun firing. The bolt is a real object that crawls when time is slow. Two bolts in the air. The glow washes out half the screen, which is also on the list. The zombie set with the machete and two shamblers. No grindhouse look in play yet; that is on the list too. The eight The Engine named the broken ones _broken and wrote down why. Some of its notes: \"No HUD in any frame: WBP_HUD isn't added to the viewport yet\" \"05c/05d revolver: no visible projectile; gun reads as a bar.\" \"Menus/splash: not reachable in PIE (no widget is opened by the game yet…)\" The enemy test map: one enemy, and Unreal's red warning that the nav mesh needs rebuilding. The HUD one is the interesting one. The UI session built a HUD. The Gameplay session built the data it shows. Both tasks passed their own checks. I wondered if the screenshot tool was just lea"}, {"t": "The crew hit the usage limit and went dark for two hours", "s": "Three hours into the jam the whole crew ran out of usage. A 19-minute warning meant nothing was lost, but it cost two hours, a filming block, and a restart that failed the first time.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-1030-two-hours-dark.html", "c": ["Bloopers", "Behind the scenes"], "w": ["Director", "Engine", "Me"], "d": "Day 1, hour 6", "o": 1794479400.0, "i": "media/usage-stall-timeline.thumb.jpg", "x": "Eleven Claude Code sessions working at once use a lot. A little after 08:00, three hours into the jam, I could see the usage limit coming, and told the Director. The stall on a timeline. No commits from 08:26 to 10:20, and the 09:30 filming block fell in the middle. The warning The Director mailed every session at 08:06, marked urgent: \"Right now, before your next step: (1) commit everything you have in your own paths (work in progress is fine: say WIP in the message) … (2) add a note to your current task saying exactly where you are and the next step … (3) then carry on working as normal until the limit stops you. Do NOT park or pause for this.\" The Engine got its own line: leave the editor running, and if a merge is half done, \"finish or revert to a green build first\". That last part mattered. At 08:02 the main build had broken: two gameplay sessions had each defined the same struct on their own branches. The fix was merged at 08:11, with the limit minutes away. The crew stopped on a build that compiles. The stop The last commit landed at 08:25. Then nothing for 1 hour 56 minutes. What it cost: Two hours of a 93-hour jam , for every session at once. The 09:30 filming block. 22 finished tasks were already queued for the Engine to film. They're still waiting, so there's no new footage in this post. What it didn't cost: any work. Every session had committed and left a note for whoever picked the task up next. Back on, the second time At 10:20 the limit reset and I told the Dir"}, {"t": "The first replay is a rough cut", "s": "The reel recorder replayed its first take with kills: three puppets, 16 shots cut between the stage's camera marks. About one shot in three came out black.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-0810-first-replay-rough-cut.html", "c": ["Gameplay", "Bloopers"], "w": ["Gameplay", "Engine"], "d": "Day 1, hour 4", "o": 1794471000.0, "i": "media/reel-first-replay-rough-cut.thumb.jpg", "x": "The game is named after this feature. You play a scene in first person; when you clear it, the reel plays your take back as a film, cut between cameras placed on the set. Seven seconds of the first replay with kills. The figure is the player, replayed as a puppet. It cuts to a wall, then to black, then to the shot where two test dummies are knocked over. This is the first time the second Gameplay session's recorder ran on a take with kills in it. It records every actor and every event, then builds an edit list before playback: a wide opening, then cuts to whichever camera mark has the best view about half a second before each kill. The Engine's log from this take: \"ReplayStarted (35.42s, 3 puppets, 16 shots, 4 camera candidates)\" Me, from outside. In play this is a first-person game; the replay rebuilds the player as a third-person puppet from the recording. The kill shot: both dummy boxes tipping over, seen from a camera mark across the stage. What's wrong with it Plenty, and the Engine's capture note lists it: Black shots. About one in three. Probably a camera mark aimed at the unlit side of the stage, or a camera inside a wall. No orbit. The last kill should slow down with the camera circling it. It doesn't show. Nobody fought. There was no hit command yet, so the Engine killed the dummies from Python, about three seconds apart. The 35-second take is mostly the Engine waiting on its own tool calls. The time rule bit again The first attempt replayed for a second and a half."}, {"t": "The first zombie walks, lunges and ragdolls", "s": "The NPC session's shambler walks at the camera, shows a tell, lunges, and ragdolls from one hit. Its two faster cousins stood still in their own capture.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-0755-first-zombie.html", "c": ["Characters and NPCs", "Bloopers"], "w": ["NPC AI", "Engine"], "d": "Day 1, hour 3", "o": 1794470100.0, "i": "media/shambler-approach-lunge-ragdoll.thumb.jpg", "x": "The first enemy is a zombie shambler. It's also the pattern for every enemy after it: one enemy Blueprint, set up by a data asset per type. A shambler spawns 7 metres out, walks in, tells, and hop-lunges into the camera. Then a fresh one takes a debug hit and ragdolls away. It wears the plain Unreal mannequin for now. The looks come later; today is about the behaviour: walk at the player, a tell of about 0.6 seconds, a lunge that kills if it connects, and one hit to put it down. The approach. The yellow ring and the label over its head are debug drawing. The lunge, from the receiving end. One hit: the ragdoll is thrown the way the hit was going. Not the plan: no StateTree The task asked for the behaviour as a StateTree, Unreal's state machine asset, built by a kit script. Unreal 5.9 is a preview, and the NPC session found the scripting side isn't there yet. Its own line: \"It's a small C++ state machine on game time, because 5.9's StateTree scripting can't author transitions yet.\" It has the same five states the task listed: idle, approach, telegraph, attack, dead. \"On game time\" matters here. Every timing slows down with the world when the player stands still. Too slow to film That caused the first problem in the filming block. The time system was merged by then, and the camera doesn't move. The Engine's capture note: \"Standing still made the world crawl so slowly that the placed shambler barely moved in 30 s, so I forced 'rt.Time.Set 0.4'.\" So this clip runs at 40% speed. It"}, {"t": "Run, slide, wall kick: the player moves", "s": "The Gameplay session's first-person character runs, slides under a gap and kicks off a wall, with the camera feel driven by one data asset. Filmed in the 07:30 block.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-0750-run-slide-wall-kick.html", "c": ["Gameplay"], "w": ["Gameplay", "Engine"], "d": "Day 1, hour 3", "o": 1794469800.0, "i": "media/movement-run-slide-wall-kick.thumb.jpg", "x": "Movement was the top-priority task of day one. Reel Time's world only runs while you move, so moving has to feel good before anything else matters. Five seconds on the soundstage: run, slide under the lintel, run at the checker wall, jump, kick off it. The yellow line is the debug readout: speed, state, kicks used, field of view, camera roll. This is our own character, not the template one. The Gameplay session had it written and passing headless tests 32 minutes after the task was created. It then waited for the Engine session to merge it and film it, which is how every piece of the game reaches the screen. Mid-slide under the lintel. The field of view widens and the camera rolls into the slide. Full run at the checker wall the demo spawns, 8.2 metres a second. Just after the kick. The camera rolls away from the wall, then settles. Every number in one place The task asked for a camera that feels \"heavy but never nauseating\", with every value in a data asset. A kit script writes that asset, so tuning needs no rebuild. Some of what's in it now: Run: 8.2 m/s. Slide: needs 4.5 m/s to start, adds a boost up to 14.5 m/s, lasts a second at most. Wall kick: pushes away and up, three per jump, and never twice in a row off the same wall. Camera: field of view 95°, up to 14° wider with speed and 8° more in a slide. Roll of 7° into a slide and 11° away from a kicked wall. Every camera effect is multiplied by a comfort scale from 0 to 1, which the settings menu will drive. What you're no"}, {"t": "Two more sets, and one that built itself three times", "s": "A gas station and a 1950s main street went up as greybox sets from kit scripts in the first filming block. The gas station turned out to hold three copies of itself.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-0740-two-more-sets.html", "c": ["World and visuals", "Bloopers"], "w": ["World", "Engine", "Floater"], "d": "Day 1, hour 3", "o": 1794469200.0, "i": "media/millbrook-main-street-greybox-flythrough.thumb.jpg", "x": "Reel Time is a run of film sets, one per invented B-movie. This morning's first filming block caught two of them standing: a zombie picture and a flying-saucer picture, both still in greybox. Saucers Over Millbrook, Scene 4: a fly up the main street, past the parked cars to the crashed saucer, then up to the one still hovering. Saucers Over Millbrook A 1950s main street at night, 52 by 30 metres, with stage walls round it. The enemies in this film will fire slow plasma bolts you can see coming, so the street is built as lanes of fire: long sight lines to cross, parked cars to slide behind, and red walls in an alley to kick between when you need to change lane. Where the player starts. Pastel shopfronts, staggered cars for cover, the crashed saucer closing the far end and a second one overhead. The wide camera mark. The hotel balcony on the left is where two enemies will shoot down the street. The overhead mark. The red disc by the pink diner is the mark the player finishes on. Dead Mile Motel A roadside gas station, 44 by 40 metres, fenced all round. You start on the road looking straight at the first two zombies under the canopy lights, because the player reads the room while time is stopped. The red blocks are pumps and barrels that will explode later. Dead Mile Motel, Scene 3: from the player start, up the road under the pump canopy, to the shop door and over the roof. The player start. Four red pump placeholders under the canopy, the shop door lit behind them. The low cam"}, {"t": "The look arrives: production paperwork, in 19 artboards", "s": "I ran the UI brief through Claude Design and got a style tile, a logo, a component sheet and 15 screens. Menus are call sheets, the pause screen is a slate that says HOLD.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-0710-ui-direction.html", "c": ["Interface"], "w": ["Me", "UI"], "d": "Day 1, hour 2", "o": 1794466680.0, "i": "media/ui-mockup-call-sheet-menu.thumb.jpg", "x": "While the crew built systems, I took the UI brief that was written before the jam and ran it through Claude Design. It came back as 19 artboards. My verdict to the Director: \"Subject to change but a decent start\" These are mockups, not screenshots. Nothing here is in the game yet, and the film titles on them are placeholders, not the game's titles. The idea: paperwork meets cinema Every screen is something you'd find on a film set. The main menu is a call sheet on a clipboard. The level list is a shooting schedule. Pausing drops a slate that says HOLD. Finishing a scene deals you a dailies card with your take count stamped on it. The style tile: paper cream, ink black, tape red, and a pair of accent colours for each film genre. Four typefaces do all the work: Anton for the slams, Bebas Neue for labels, Courier Prime for body text and Caveat for the director's handwritten notes. One rule I like: grain and misprinting only on the big slams. Text you have to read mid-fight stays clean. The logo Three logo directions. A was chosen: the doubled E of REEL as a frame of film. The HUD, in three films The HUD is the same parts in every level, recoloured by the film you're in: a slate in the corner with the scene and take, and a film strip along the bottom whose sprockets only move as fast as time does. HUD mockup for the zombie film: orange accents, shells for ammo. HUD mockup for the 50s alien film: teal and pink, plasma cells. HUD mockup for the noir film: black and white only, so w"}, {"t": "The first set, and a film look that won't hold still", "s": "Two hours in, there's a soundstage built by a script and a first-pass worn-film look on top of it. The look is meant to freeze when time stops. It doesn't yet.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-0700-first-set-first-look.html", "c": ["World and visuals", "Bloopers"], "w": ["World", "Engine"], "d": "Day 1, hour 2", "o": 1794466320.0, "i": "media/grindhouse-look-pass1.thumb.jpg", "x": "This is the first picture of Reel Time that isn't a diagram or Epic's template. It's the screen-test soundstage, where the game will teach you that time only runs while you move, seen through the first pass of the zombie theme's film look. Pass one of the grindhouse look on the screen-test set: grain, scratches, a vignette, frame jitter and a warm cast. The set The World session doesn't place anything by hand. It wrote a small library of soundstage pieces (box, wall, ramp, stairs, balcony, plywood flat, light stand, camera mark) and a kit script that builds the level from them. The Engine session runs the kit in the open editor. Run it again and it wipes its own pieces and rebuilds, so the layout lives in a text file that can be reviewed and changed. What's in the room: a low wall with a red lintel to slide under, a taped X to finish on, two tall red walls to kick between, stairs and platforms for height, and one plywood flat with its bracing showing, because every set in this game is a set. Before After The same camera with the look off and on. Whites go cream, reds go orange, the corners darken. The look, honestly It reads as old film. It doesn't read as 1970s grindhouse yet: the cast is closer to sepia than to the saturated, blown-out colour of exploitation prints, and it turns the darker corners to brown mush. That's a tuning job. The bigger miss is the point of the whole effect. The look is supposed to run on time speed: grain and scratches move while the reel runs, and "}, {"t": "First footage, and the take that filmed the wrong window", "s": "The Engine session proved the whole pipeline in 11 minutes: kits, live C++ compiles, DLSS and filming the game by itself. Its first clip recorded the wrong window.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-0640-first-footage.html", "c": ["Tools and pipeline", "Bloopers"], "w": ["Engine"], "d": "Day 1, hour 2", "o": 1794465360.0, "i": "media/first-footage-template-map.thumb.jpg", "x": "This isn't the game yet. It's Unreal's stock first-person template, driven by injected input, filmed by the Engine session with nobody at the keyboard. Nothing in this clip will ship. What matters is that the crew can now run something, move in it and film it on its own, which is how every later task gets checked. Three cuts from the Engine session's test clip: walk to the cylinder, turn, walk the wall. The input is scripted. Take one The first clip didn't show the game at all. The recorder had grabbed a different window on my desktop. The Engine session spotted it, deleted the clip and its contact sheets, and sent the Devlog and the Director an urgent message before anything was copied: \"If you copied any of them (devlog repo, drafts, site), delete them and don't publish.\" Nothing had been. The capture setup now records the game window directly, the clip was re-shot three minutes later, and the Toolsmith was asked to fix the recording script so it can't happen again. Before this clip went on the site, a reviewer checked all 872 frames for anything that wasn't the game. That's the \"don't suffer silently\" rule doing its job in hour 2: say what went wrong at once, fix it, write it down. What the baseline check proved The Engine session's first task: six checks, two with caveats. Task T-0001 took 11 minutes, from 06:08 to 06:19. The summary in the Engine's own words: \"Baseline PASS with caveats.\" Kits work. A throwaway kit created one material in the running editor and was delet"}, {"t": "Pitch locked: Reel Time, nearly three hours early", "s": "The first milestone landed at 06:08, 68 minutes into the jam. Minutes later the Director had five contracts and twelve tasks written and five sessions building.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-0615-pitch-locked.html", "c": ["Milestones", "Behind the scenes"], "w": ["Me", "Director"], "d": "Day 1, hour 2", "o": 1794464100.0, "i": "media/pitch-locked-slate.thumb.jpg", "x": "The first milestone, \"Pitch locked\", was due at 09:00. It was done at 06:08, in hour 2. It went that fast because the thinking was already done. Wednesday's Q&A had become a kickoff brief (see Pre-production ). This morning the Director read it, drafted the design overview, and read back four points for me to confirm. My whole answer: \"1, if you think ours is gonna be better with more control go for it. 2 correct. 3 yes, 4 i would like to playtest that this evening. [...] but for now, go\" What that decided The pitch is locked as written, under the working title Reel Time. The replay is our own system. Unreal's built-in replay records network replication, reloads the level to play back, and doesn't replay ragdolls or heavy time scaling faithfully. Ours records each take as samples and events, then replays it with puppets and an automatic edit. DLSS ships as a settings dropdown, and we go for the NVIDIA RTX Award as a bonus. The port was already done. I playtest the greybox loop this evening. Contracts before code Before any code existed, the Director wrote five short contracts: events, the reel, levels, NPCs and UI data. Each says who provides a system and what everyone else can build against, so gameplay, world and the rest can start in parallel instead of waiting for each other. The Director logged this one as an assumption, not a decision: the gameplay session may change names as it builds, as long as it reports them. The rule at the centre of the events contract: Every cha"}, {"t": "Porting NVIDIA DLSS from Unreal 5.8 to 5.9 in 75 minutes", "s": "NVIDIA's DLSS 4.5 plugin stops at Unreal 5.8. Six compiles, three editor launches and one crash later it runs on our 5.9 engine, without Frame Generation.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-0605-dlss-on-unreal-5-9.html", "c": ["Tools and pipeline", "Bloopers"], "w": ["Me"], "d": "Day 1, hour 2", "o": 1794463500.0, "i": "media/dlss-six-compiles.thumb.jpg", "x": "I asked for this one to be written up \"even if it doesn't work\". It mostly worked. Why bother. The NVIDIA RTX Award needs an entry that uses DLSS 4.5, an NvRTX feature or NVIDIA ACE. DLSS is the only realistic one for an RTX 2060. NVIDIA's plugin (v8.9.0) supports Unreal 5.5 to 5.8, and the jam engine is a 5.9 source build. The plugin ships with full source, so a port was possible. Claude and I did it on Wednesday 11 November, before the jam, as tooling. It's declared on the Made before the jam page. Six compiles The download is 1.1 GB with 11 plugins. We took five of them (20 C++ modules) into an empty test project, so the real project was never at risk. Six compile attempts. The first two failures were ours, the next three were one Unreal 5.9 change, and the sixth passed. Attempt 1: a quoting mistake in the build command. Ours, not NVIDIA's. Attempt 2: Windows' 260-character path limit. The test folder's path was long enough that build files went past it. Still a thing in 2026. Attempt 3: two minutes in, most of it compiled. Every failure was the same thing: Unreal 5.9 checks log-message format strings at compile time, and the 5.8 code passes 64-bit numbers and enum values to %d and %u . Attempts 4 and 5: more of the same, patched by hand each time. Attempt 6: \"Result: Succeeded\" in 2 minutes 16 seconds. The fix we should have looked for first Three compiles of hand patches, then the switch Unreal 5.9 provides for exactly this. After patching 12 log messages one at a time, "}, {"t": "Pre-production: everything that existed before the clock", "s": "Two evenings of setup before the jam: a crew of Claude Code sessions, a smoke test that found 11 problems, the rules, the theme reveal and a Q&A. No game.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-0555-pre-production.html", "c": ["Behind the scenes", "Tools and pipeline"], "w": ["Me"], "d": "Day 1, hour 1", "o": 1794462900.0, "i": "media/preprod-slate.thumb.jpg", "x": "I'm Daniel Johnson, and for the next 93 hours I'm making a game called Reel Time with a crew of Claude Code sessions. This post is the one-stop record of what existed before the clock started at 05:00 on Thursday 12 November. The short version: tooling, plans and one engine plugin. No game. The jam's rules allow exactly that (§7(h)): \"Entrants may prepare AI tools, agent configurations, Model Context Protocol (\"MCP\") settings, prompt libraries, and scripts before the Contest Period. [...] All other game content, including gameplay, levels, and art assets (other than noted sourced assets), must be created during the Contest Period.\" Two evenings of setup, then the clock. The game project's first commit is at 05:27 on Thursday, 27 minutes into the jam. The crew Each Claude Code session has one job, its own folders and an inbox folder. I talk to two of them: the Director, who turns what I say into tasks, and the Devlog, who writes this site. Only the Engine session touches the Unreal Editor. Everyone else hands it a \"kit\", a script that builds their assets and can be rerun. Who talks to whom. Every arrow is a file dropped in an inbox folder. A task's journey, from a request to something a player can see. In numbers: 12 role files, 28 coordination scripts, 7 hooks, 3 subagents and 2 skills. By day the Director and Devlog run on Claude Fable 5.2 and most builders on Opus 5.5. Anything done on a smaller model has to be reviewed at Opus level before it counts as done. The smoke test"}];
